-- A particle can be sent to three places, and all three answer the same way
-- when they cannot do the work: with an `Exception` as their result. The
-- frame that emitted carries on and decides what to do with it.
--
-- `to this` and `to <module>` have worked this way since phases 4 and 2.
-- `core` did not: phase 3 wired its failures into the flag channel, which
-- made them unwind the *caller* instead of answering — so `emit Double { }
-- to math get r` bound `r` while `emit Length { } to core get r` ended the
-- frame outright. Fixed 2026-08-28: `core` is a recipient like any other,
-- which is what `dispatch_core` already claimed for the class-it-doesn't-know
-- case.
link "native_modules/math.so" as math

Bad { } => {
    return 42
}

Viacore { } => {
    emit Length { } to core get r
    return Reached { "r": r }
}
Viamodule { } => {
    emit Double { } to math get r
    return Reached { "r": r }
}
Viahandler { } => {
    emit Bad { } to this get r
    return Reached { "r": r }
}

emit Viacore { } to this get a
emit Viamodule { } to this get b
emit Viahandler { } to this get c

-- The point: every one of them got past the emit.
assert a is Reached
assert b is Reached
assert c is Reached

assert a.r is Exception
assert b.r is Exception
assert c.r is Exception

-- And they name who could not do it, which is the one thing that differs.
assert a.r.source = "core"
assert b.r.source = "math"
assert c.r.source = "core"
